      "hypothesis": "Round-0 evidence shows that a fast short-horizon reversal sleeve dominates the composite and loses badly under open-to-open execution while generating about 0.65 daily turnover and 0.49 cumulative cost; the lagged close-based reversal is likely monetizing close-auction/bid-ask effects that are not earned from the next open onward. I therefore remove short-horizon reversal entirely and build a slower dollar-neutral book: 12-1 and 6-1 momentum (open-to-open, skipping the most recent month, smoothed over 5 sessions) provide cross-sectional continuation; a 20% low-volatility tilt adds defensiveness and is cheap to trade. I select the most liquid tradable names (price > $1, 60-day median dollar volume >= $1M, eligible flag), rank on a robust z-score composite, go equal-weight long the top 50 and short the bottom 50 (or fewer if liquidity is limited) with per-name weight capped at 2% and gross at about 0.98, reducing turnover relative to round 0.",
